Hi Marc,

I believe the problem can be fixed by providing both "x" and "y" to the model as data rather than as constants. I can't speak to why this was working earlier, but I was able to resolve the problem and get reasonable sampling by making that change.

Luca, dnorm() by default uses precision, but NIMBLE allows named arguments to distributions. So y ~ dnorm(0, 0.01) is equivalent to y ~ dnorm(0, sd = 10). If you're ever stumped on what a distribution's defaults and alternative parameterizations are you can check out page 2 of the NIMBLE cheat sheet <https://r-nimble.org/cheatsheets/NimbleCheatSheet.pdf>!

Ben

